The single architectural story your document should tell

The whole document should ultimately lead the reviewer to this:

CURRENT

Business Transaction

Client → IIB → IBM MQ → Business Processing → SUCCESS

Logging Workload

IIB → IBM MQ → Oracle

The business transaction does not depend on Oracle logging success, but the logging workload creates very high Oracle write concurrency.

TARGET

Client → IIB → IBM MQ → Business Processing → SUCCESS

and independently:

Logging Events → Kafka →

Oracle Consumer → Oracle

Data Lake Consumer → Iceberg/Parquet

Analytics Consumer → Trino/Spark

Future Consumer → AI/Other

with:

Kafka → Retry → DLQ

and separately:

Oracle → Redo → GoldenGate → CDC

That is, in my view, the corrected architectural narrative for your actual EIS behavior. It preserves your MQ, introduces Kafka for scalable downstream processing, and—most importantly—does not incorrectly claim that Oracle logging controls business transaction success. 
